  "hypothesis_title": "52-week-high proximity (anchoring under-reaction) — George-Hwang 2004",
  "hypothesis_description": "George-Hwang (2004): investors anchor on the salient 52-week high; good news is under-reacted when price is near the anchor, producing subsequent positive drift. Signal = Close_t / max(High,252d) (price LEVEL relative to anchor), cross-sectional z, sector-neutralized, t-1 close, 2e8 liquidity. Mechanism is a price-level-vs-anchor effect, designed distinct from raw/residual momentum (function of past returns) and from short-term reversal (sign-flipped past 1M return).",
  "selection_objective": "rank_ic",
  "model_type": "linear_cross_sectional_anchoring_level",
  "economic_rationale": "Behavioral anchoring + under-reaction (George-Hwang 2004; Grinblatt-Han 2005 disposition adjacency). Anchor = 52w high; nearness proxies how 'capped' good news is. Distinct economic channel from risk-premium momentum.",
